From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: server: user interface privilege isolation -- the session cannot message an elevated program

An elevated program (ADR 0012) runs as SYSTEM, in the same wineserver as the
user's session. sg-compositor and sg-elevated-run give it an X display the
session cannot reach (bug B56), but the server itself was a second way in:
any program in the session could look up the elevated window's handle and
post it WM_KEYDOWN, WM_CHAR, WM_COMMAND or WM_PASTE, attach its thread's input
state, or set a hook on its thread -- which loads the hook's DLL into the
elevated process. Windows closes exactly this with UIPI.

A process below high integrity may now send a process at high integrity or
above (an administrator's elevated program, SYSTEM) only what UIPI lets
through: WM_NULL, WM_MOVE, WM_SIZE, WM_GETTEXT, WM_GETTEXTLENGTH,
WM_GETHOTKEY, WM_GETICON, WM_RENDERFORMAT, WM_DRAWCLIPBOARD, WM_CHANGECBCHAIN,
WM_THEMECHANGED, and the shared clipboard's notifications to its owner and
viewers. Anything else fails with access denied, as SendMessage and
PostMessage fail there. AttachThreadInput between the two, and a thread hook
set from below on an elevated thread, are refused the same way. Global hooks
are per desktop, and an elevated program has a desktop of its own.

Scoped to the administrator boundary on purpose: low and medium integrity
processes (a browser's sandboxed children and their parent) message each
other as before. Higher to lower is unchanged, as on Windows.

The clipboard stays shared per window station, as on Windows: an elevated
program reads it when it pastes (its user's own action); nothing below can
make it paste (WM_PASTE is refused).

Forwarded: not-needed
